                "text": "Let me understand the rule. Background orange=7. Magenta clusters=6. Recolor by shape/size mapping in verifier:\n- size 2 \u2192 9 (maroon)\n- size 3 \u2192 straight? 2 (red) : 4 (yellow)\n- size 4 \u2192 8 (azure)\n- size 5 \u2192 3 (green)\n- size 6 \u2192 5 (grey)\n\nI need to create 2 demonstration input grids that the verifier can evaluate, and show_transformed_input will compute the output.\n\nKey: the student sees only my 2 pairs. I should make them informative and cover as many shape types as possible. Let me design grids with distinct clusters covering all six categories: plus(5), Z/S(4), vertical line 3, vertical line 2, L-tromino 3, 2x3 rectangle 6.\n\nBut careful: each input is a single 9x9 grid with possibly multiple clusters. I can put multiple clusters in one grid as long as they're not orthogonally connected. Let me create two grids, each having several clusters.\n\nActually the mapping is based purely on size and straightness, not on exact shape. So the student can infer: size2\u21929, size3 straight\u21922, size3 non-straight\u21924, size4\u21928, size5\u21923, size6\u21925. To teach this, I need examples covering these sizes and straightness cases.\n\nLet me build grid 1 covering: a plus (size5), a vertical line of 3 (straight size3), a vertical line of 2 (size2), and a 2x3 rectangle (size6). That's 4 clusters.\n\nGrid 2: a Z tetromino (size4), an L tromino (size3 non-straight), plus maybe another size2 and size5 to reinforce.\n\nLet me carefully place them on 9x9 with background 7.\n\nGrid 1:\n- Plus at rows 1-3, cols 1-3: center (2,2), cells (1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,2). Size5.\n- Vertical line 3 at col 5, rows 1-3: (1,5),(2,5),(3,5). Size3 straight\u21922.\n- Vertical line 2 at col 7, rows 1-2: (1,7),(2,7). Size2\u21929.\n- 2x3 rectangle: rows 5-6, cols 1-3: (5,1),(5,2),(5,3),(6,1),(6,2),(6,3). Size6\u21925.\n\nCheck no orthogonal connections between clusters. Plus at cols1-3 rows1-3; rect at rows5-6 cols1-3, gap row4.